lines+=['Only five universes underpin these intervals; they do not cover every roster distribution, season or parameter uncertainty. The same retained seed family is used for comparability, not a fresh out-of-sample validation set.','','2. Roster allocation','The paired candidate widens initial fictional NBA team quality by about 15% through deterministic same-position player swaps. It preserves the entire league-wide player pool, IDs, names, attributes, salaries and positional counts. It does not inflate individual ratings or move players in existing saves. Applied only during new male-league fictional generation for 2020 onward. The target is preseason quality spread; season margin SD is measured afterward, never forced.','The first pilot chose tiny swaps and failed to produce the intended spread. Its three completed seasons and interrupted runs are retained separately and excluded from acceptance. The corrected roster allocation passes identity/attribute preservation checks for five seeds. One to four swaps reach the target in those fixtures.','','3. Points-per-possession distribution and transition mechanism','Real comparison: 134 of 136 retained official NBA 2024–25 PBP games pass score reconciliation; two score-correction files are excluded. This is an availability sample, not a complete season or guaranteed random sample. Offensive rebounds continue possessions; opposing offensive actions or defensive rebounds switch control; period ends close possession. Technical free throws are separate administrative points. Team rebounds, retained-ball violations and official possession conventions can still differ.','Simulation comparison: 120 reset games per arm, world 604 and matching trial seeds. Full possessions include secondary shots and free throws. Simulation totals reconcile to final scores. Simulation technical points remain attached to their execution slot, so the PPP definitions are close but not identical.','Points | Real reconstructed % | Phase 8 % | Paired candidate %']
